Customisations applied:
  - Re-authored the agent-style upstream ("You are a specialist in Azure DNS", numbered
    task-approach steps, imperative sections) into vault operator prose: declarative
    descriptions, classify-first framing, no second-person agent instructions.
  - Four-field vault frontmatter (name, description, license MIT, metadata.version 1.0.0).
  - Keyword-rich CSO description enumerating both references, trigger keyphrases covering
    the full Azure DNS surface (public zones, private zones, Private Resolver, Alias records,
    DNSSEC, Traffic Manager, Firewall DNS proxy, IaC, CLI), and boundary callouts to
    dns-network-ops and azure-cloud-ops.
  - Skill marker in SKILL.md only; references carry no frontmatter, no skill marker; line 1
    is the # Title in each reference.
  - Split the source material into two focused references: architecture.md (build, configure,
    IaC) and diagnostics.md (troubleshoot, diagnose, fix). The upstream SKILL.md's "Common
    Pitfalls" section was substantially expanded into a full diagnostics reference with
    per-failure-type diagnostic flows and CLI commands.
  - Added the "Pattern 3: Hub-spoke with centralised DNS" hybrid pattern (present in
    architecture diagram in upstream references/architecture.md but not narrated in upstream
    SKILL.md).
  - Added the RBAC section (not present in upstream; standard Azure DNS operational need).
  - Added a structured "classify first" table (zone type vs public/private resolvability).
  - Upstream source used em dashes (--) as ASCII double-hyphen in text; zero em-dash (U+2014)
    or en-dash (U+2013) glyphs across all output files. ASCII "->" used for flow arrows;
    commas, semicolons, and parentheses for dash-style pauses.
  - British/Pacific English applied: authorisation, centralised, behaviour, recognised.
  - Private endpoint DNS zone-name table and the "wrong privatelink zone name" red flag added
    (partially in upstream "Common Pitfalls" item 7, expanded with a full table in architecture.md
    and a diagnostic check in diagnostics.md).
  - DNS forwarding rule trailing-dot requirement called out in both SKILL.md red flags and
    diagnostics.md common-errors table (a frequently missed operational detail).

Boundary notes:
  azure-dns-ops = AZURE PLATFORM DNS IMPLEMENTATION (public zones, private zones, Private
  Resolver, Alias records, DNSSEC, Traffic Manager, Firewall DNS proxy, IaC, CLI).
  dns-network-ops owns DNS architecture, DNSSEC design (KSK/ZSK strategy, NSEC vs NSEC3,
  algorithm selection, cross-platform comparison, hybrid DNS patterns at the conceptual level).
  azure-cloud-ops owns broader Azure platform operations (compute, IAM, cost management,
  non-DNS networking) that accompany but are outside DNS.
  network-source-of-truth owns IPAM and the DNS record-of-intent for zones this skill operates.
  No content duplication with dns-network-ops: this skill covers Azure-specific implementation;
  conceptual and cross-platform material routes to dns-network-ops.

